.TH MAN 1
.SH NAME
man \- print sections of the manual
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B man
[\ section\ ] name ...
.br
.B man \-k
keyword ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B man
prints the page of the manual that describes each
.IR name :
a command, or a function of the libraries. The manual is in sections:
.TP 6
.B 1
commands
.TP
.B 2
system calls
.TP
.B 3
the functions of the libraries
.PP
A
.I section
number before the names restricts man to that section; otherwise the
page shown is the first one found, looking in section 1, then 2, and so
on. A name may be that of the page, or of any of the commands or
functions a page describes:
.B "man strcpy"
prints the page string(3), which describes strcpy among others.
.PP
On a terminal, the page is shown through
.BR more (1):
the space bar shows the next screen, the return key the next line, and
.B q
stops. When the output is not a terminal, the page is copied to it as
it is, as in
.BR "man cc > cc.txt" .
.PP
.B "man \-k"
prints the one-line summary of every page whose names or description
contain one of the
.IR keyword s,
in upper or lower case:
.PP
.nf
	$ man \-k directory
.fi
.PP
The pages are kept formatted, as plain text, since XENIX has no text
formatter to make them each time they are asked for.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 10
.B MANPATH
a directory to look for the pages in, instead of /usr/man
.SH FILES
.TP 24
/usr/man/cat\fIN\fP/\fIpage\fP.\fIN\fP
the pages of section N, formatted
.TP
/usr/man/whatis
the index: each page, the names it describes, and its summary
.TP
/usr/bin/more
the pager
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"no manual entry for
.IR name \(rq
when there is no page for it; "no entry for
.I name
in section
.IR N \(rq
when there is none in that section. The exit status is 1 if any page
was not found, and 0 otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
more(1), intro(1), intro(3)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B man
command first appeared in Version 2 AT&T UNIX. The
.B \-k
option first appeared in 4.0BSD.
.SH AUTHORS
This man was written for the xenix86-devsys Development System.
.SH BUGS
There are pages only for the Development System: the commands and
system calls of the Operating System have none.
